 To automatically prefix a string before using it as a regular expression in various programming languages, you can concatenate the prefix with the regular expression pattern. Here's an example in JavaScript:

```javascript
const prefix = 'auto-'; // your desired prefix
const regexPattern = /your-regex-pattern/; // your existing regular expression pattern

// Prefix the regex pattern with the given prefix
const prefixRegex = new RegExp(`${prefix}${regexPattern.source}`);

// Now use the prefixed regex
const result = prefixRegex.test('inputString');
console.log(result);
```

In this example, replace `'auto-'` with your desired prefix and `/your-regex-pattern/` with your existing regular expression pattern. This way, when you create a new `RegExp` object, the prefix is added to the beginning of the pattern.

Keep in mind that different programming languages may have slightly different syntax for creating regular expressions, but the general idea remains the same - concatenate the prefix with the existing regular expression pattern.

