 I'd be happy to help you understand the issue you're experiencing with including an Authorization header in your request, given the context you've provided.

The 'Access-Control-Allow-Origin' header is used by servers to indicate which origins (domain names and schemes) are allowed to access the resources on the server. When this header is set to '*' or a wildcard ('*'), it means that any origin is allowed to access the server's resources. This does not directly prevent you from sending an Authorization header with your request.

However, there could be other reasons why your request is not including the Authorization header. One common reason is due to the same-origin policy enforced by web browsers for security reasons. If you are making a cross-origin request (i.e., from a different domain), the browser will only send the Origin header, but not the Authorization header, unless the server explicitly allows it through the Access-Control-Allow-Headers header.

To troubleshoot further, you can check the following:

1. Ensure that the server is returning the Access-Control-Allow-Headers header in its response, and that it includes "Authorization". For example, if you're making a GET request, you can use the developer tools in your browser to inspect the server's response headers.
2. Check your client-side code to ensure that you're correctly setting the Authorization header in your request. For example, in JavaScript using fetch API:

```javascript
const myHeaders = new Headers();
myHeaders.append('Authorization', 'Bearer <your_token>');
fetch('https://example.com/api/resource', {
  method: 'GET',
  headers: myHeaders
})
.then(response => {
  // handle response
})
.catch(error => {
  // handle error
});
```

3. Make sure that your token or authentication mechanism is valid and being passed correctly in the Authorization header.
4. If you're testing your application locally, note that most web browsers do not support CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) for localhost requests. In that case, you may need to run your server on a publicly accessible domain or use a proxy server to make the request.

